  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nAt 12 PM the main rainfall band was west of Los Angeles County. Rain \nis expected to continue to move south and east, reaching Orange \nCounty late this afternoon and San Diego County early this evening. \nTwo main bands of precipitation are expected with the first one at \nthe onset of precipitation and the next early Thursday morning. \nInstability will increase overnight as colder air aloft moves across \nrecord warm coastal waters for this time in October, around 7 \ndegrees warmer than the long-term average. While coverage of \nprecipitation may be less overnight, there is greater potential for \nbrief higher-intensity rainfall from isolated thunderstorms and \nstronger showers.\n\nAdditional showers will continue on Thursday along with a slight \nchance of thunderstorms during the afternoon. The greater coverage \nis expected for Orange and southwestern San Bernardino Counties from \nthe coast to the mountains. The upper level flow will turn \nnorthwesterly Thursday evening, with scattered showers continuing \nfor San Diego County into Friday morning, with drier conditions \nfurther north. \n\nThrough Thursday night for Orange and southwestern San Bernardino \nCounties, rainfall is expected to range from 0.50-0.75 inch near the \ncoast to 1 to 1.5 inches for the San Bernardino County mountains. \nFor San Diego County, rainfall will decrease from north to south \nwith 0.75 to locally 1 inch over northwestern San Diego County to \n0.25 to 0.50 inch across far southern San Diego County. For the most \npart, rainfall rates will peak at 0.20-30 inches per hour, with the \npotential for rates 0.45-0.70 inches where thunderstorms develop.  \n\nHigh temperatures will run 5 degrees below average along the coast \nThursday and 10 to 20 degrees below average inland. Temperatures \nwill gradually warm Friday onward. For next Monday, high \ntemperatures for coastal areas will be 5 to locally 10 degrees above \naverage with the mountains and deserts a few to around 5 degrees \nbelow average. Additionally, the flow will turn offshore on \nSaturday. Gusty northeast to east winds will develop in and below \nmountain passes and canyons as well as the Inland Empire corridor \nbetween the Cajon Pass and the Santa Ana Mountains. The offshore \nflow weakens for Sunday into early next week.",
